<h1>Streaming</h1>
<p class="lead">
  Falcon runs each request in its own fiber, so a route waiting on IO (a query, an HTTP call, a <code>sleep</code>)
  lets the others run. The routes are in <code>demos/controllers/streaming_demo.rb</code>.
</p>

<section>
  <h2>A response streamed as it's written</h2>
  <p><code>stream do |out| ... end</code> sends each write as it's made. This reads <code>/streaming/countdown</code> as it arrives.</p>
  <button data-run="countdown">Start the countdown</button>
  <pre id="countdown" class="output"></pre>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Concurrent work, with Async::Barrier</h2>
  <p>
    Three lookups of 0.9s, 0.7s, and 0.5s run at once, so they take about 0.9s, not 2.1s. Each reaches the request
    through <code>Reprised.current</code>, from inside its own task.
  </p>
  <button data-run="parallel">Run the lookups</button>
  <pre id="parallel" class="output"></pre>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Route timeouts</h2>
  <p>
    <code>get "/streaming/slow", timeout: 1</code> stops the route after a second, raising <code>Reprised::Timeout</code>
    (a 503), which the controller's error handler answers.
  </p>
  <button data-run="slow">Take 3 seconds</button>
  <button data-run="slow" data-query="seconds=0.5">Take 0.5 seconds</button>
  <pre id="slow" class="output"></pre>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>A stall</h2>
  <p>
    Busy work that never waits on IO holds up every request in the process. Start the <a href="/events" target="_blank">clock</a>
    in another tab, then block the event loop for 1.5s: the clock stops, and the server's log has the stall
    detector's report, naming this request.
  </p>
  <button data-run="block">Block the event loop</button>
  <pre id="block" class="output"></pre>
</section>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll("[data-run]").forEach((button) => {
    button.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      const name = button.dataset.run;
      const output = document.getElementById(name);
      const started = performance.now();
      output.textContent = "";
      const response = await fetch(`/streaming/${name}${button.dataset.query ? `?${button.dataset.query}` : ""}`);
      const reader = response.body.pipeThrough(new TextDecoderStream()).getReader();
      for (;;) {
        const { value, done } = await reader.read();
        if (done) break;
        output.textContent += value;
      }
      output.textContent += `\n(${response.status}, ${((performance.now() - started) / 1000).toFixed(2)}s)`;
    });
  });
</script>
